1. Review & Validation of Point 1 (The 185+ Excel Columns)
You are completely right on your observation, and there is a very specific technical reason why you are seeing this:
Why only columns J, K, L, M, O, P, Q, BE, BF, BG, BI, BK match directly:
These represent single-value, core entities at the Account/Policy level in PolicyCenter:
Account/Contact: Account Name, Broker/Producer, Broker City, Broker State, Insured State.
Classification/Industry: SIC, NAICS Sector/Subsector/Code (IndustryCode), Type of Business (ToB), ISO Dominant Subclass (ClassCode).
Basic Terms: Attachment Point, Policy Number, Business Unit.
Why the remaining ~185 columns are NOT direct fields on PolicyCenter tables:
Horizontal Pivot vs. Normalized Array (The Big Reason): If you inspect columns BM through HF in the Excel sheet, the spreadsheet repeats layer attributes across columns:
Layer 1: Coverage Type, Occ Limit, Agg Limit, Attachment, SIR/Ded, Proposed Program Layer, Premium, Carrier.
Layer 2: Repeated set of 10+ columns.
Layer 3 through Layer 14 (Umbrella, 1st Excess, 2nd Excess, etc.): Repeated sets of columns.
In Guidewire PolicyCenter (Excess/Surplus & Commercial Lines), layers and underlying coverages are never stored as flat horizontal columns (like LimitLayer1, LimitLayer2). Instead, they are stored as one-to-many child entity arrays (e.g., PolicyLine.Coverages, CoverageLayers, or custom entities like CoverageLayer_Ext / UnderlyingCoverage_Ext).
Pricing/Actuarial Model vs. Guidewire System of Record: Many columns (such as Below 1B, Below 75M Attach, Ded >= $250, MechPrem, RRL, Proposed Total Excess ProgramCarrier) are underwriting workbook/pricing model fields. They were derived or keyed inside offline pricing models, not native OOTB PolicyCenter database fields.
Conclusion: We must confirm with the BA/Business whether these fields are currently captured anywhere in the PolicyCenter UI, or if they are expecting fields that only exist in their external pricing tools.

